gainst the standard pwd command. I'm looking at the requirements and design decisions for implementing the readlink utility with features R1.1 through R1.4, including setting up signal handling, using the standard library's readlink function, and running differential tests against greadlink to verify correctness. The next phase will build on this foundation to add the remaining features R1.5-R1.6 and R2.1-R2.2. I'm laying out the requirements for implementing features R1.5, R1.6, R2.1, and R2.2 in the readlink command, with plans to modify the main implementation and test files accordingly. The acceptance criteria focus on compilation, correct feature behavior, and matching the output of greadlink across all test cases. I'm looking at the specific requirements and acceptance criteria for this task—implementing four features (R3.1, R3.2, R4.1, R4.2) in the readlink command, extending the existing flag parsing logic, and adding differential test cases to verify everything works correctly.\n\nBut I'm second-guessing whether my requirement descriptions are too generic. The thing is, the stitch agent will have access to the SRD document itself, so referencing requirements by ID is the right approach—I don't need to duplicate that content. What matters is giving enough context about what functionality each requirement covers so the agent can plan effectively.\n\nLooking at the roadmap description and the SRD IDs, I can map out what each group likely covers: core readlink behavior with the different modes, multi-argument handling and error cases, additional options, and then exit codes and standard behaviors. The format of just saying \"Implement srd050-readlink requirement R1.1\" works fine because the agent will look up the actual specification. I should focus on making the requirement descriptions indicate the general functional area rather than trying to restate the SRD content. I'm stuck on how to handle the SRD requirement mapping without being able to read the actua